<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  com.gftd.orgs-detector-tick — the scheduler for detectors that CANNOT be
  fleet gates, because a fleet gate ships only the target repo's own tree and
  they read `orgs/`.

  =========================================================================
  THIS IS NOT INSTALLED, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
  =========================================================================

  ADR-2608124800 counted five plists sitting in `scripts/` uninstalled, one of
  them the only caller of three verifiers. So "another plist in scripts/" is
  exactly the shape of the failure this whole change is about, and shipping a
  sixth one silently would repeat it.

  The difference is that this time the mechanism does not depend on the plist.
  Run the tick by hand and the SessionStart hook reports it; never run it and
  the hook says, in the first line of every session, that it has never run.
  The scheduling decision is the owner's, and the honest statement of what
  installing it would mean is below, in full, so that decision can be made from
  this file alone.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  EXACTLY WHAT WOULD RUN
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

    /opt/homebrew/bin/nbb --classpath ".:scripts/nbb_compat" \
      scripts/orgs-detector-tick.cljs

  with WorkingDirectory /Users/junkawasaki/github/com-junkawasaki.

  It reads `manifest/orgs-detectors.edn` and runs each detector whose own
  `:interval-ms` has elapsed. As registered today that is two:

    verify-vendored-copies   every  6h   measured  1m41s   34 vendored files
    verify-require-graph     every 24h   measured  8m00s   3571 repos w/ src root

  Worst case one firing does both: ~11 minutes at 29% of one core (it is
  dominated by subprocesses, and this machine runs many concurrent agents).
  Typical firing is the 6h one alone, ~2 minutes. `StartInterval` below is
  hourly because the per-detector intervals do the real scheduling -- an hourly
  wake that finds nothing due exits in well under a second.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  EXACTLY WHAT IT TOUCHES
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  WRITES, only these, all outside every git checkout:

    ~/.gftd/orgs-detector-tick/state.edn        the finding set + first-seen
    ~/.gftd/orgs-detector-tick/state.edn.lock   one run at a time
    ~/.gftd/orgs-detector-tick/tick.log         append-only run log

  READS: `orgs/**` checkouts, `manifest/orgs-detectors.edn`, and for
  verify-vendored-copies `git show`/`git log`/`git rev-list` inside already
  checked-out library repos.

  WRITES TO NO CHECKOUT, INCLUDING THIS ONE. It never fetches, never checks
  out, never moves HEAD. Detectors run with GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=none, so a
  `git fetch`/`clone`/`push` inside one fails at the transport layer instead of
  succeeding quietly, and with GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0 so read commands do not
  refresh an index. That is transport hardening, not a sandbox: a local
  `git checkout` or a stray write is held off by review at admission time, and
  the tick's header says so rather than implying more.

  NO NETWORK of its own, no credentials, no `gh`. Detectors that need those are
  deliberately not in the registry -- see the registry header for which and why.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHAT INSTALLING IT CHANGES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  It changes only how often the numbers are refreshed. It does not change what
  is reported, where, or to whom: the SessionStart hook reads the same state
  file either way, and reports a stale tick as loudly as a finding. If this is
  never installed, the tick is a hand-run command whose results survive between
  sessions -- which is already strictly better than the detectors' status quo
  of being run by nobody.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  install / uninstall / run once
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

    cp scripts/com.gftd.orgs-detector-tick.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
    launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.gftd.orgs-detector-tick.plist
    launchctl bootout   gui/$(id -u)/com.gftd.orgs-detector-tick
    launchctl kickstart -p gui/$(id -u)/com.gftd.orgs-detector-tick

  ProcessType Standard, not Background: measured on this machine 2026-07-25, a
  Background+Nice job got 0.09s of CPU in 15 minutes while other sessions held
  the CPU. RunAtLoad false so installing it does not immediately start an
  11-minute sweep.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.gftd.orgs-detector-tick</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/opt/homebrew/bin/nbb</string>
    <string>--classpath</string>
    <string>.:scripts/nbb_compat</string>
    <string>scripts/orgs-detector-tick.cljs</string>
  </array>

  <!-- nbb resolves `--classpath .` against the working directory, and the
       detectors resolve `orgs/` against it too. -->
  <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
  <string>/Users/junkawasaki/github/com-junkawasaki</string>

  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin</string>
    <key>HOME</key>
    <string>/Users/junkawasaki</string>
  </dict>

  <key>StartInterval</key>
  <integer>3600</integer>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <false/>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/Users/junkawasaki/.gftd/orgs-detector-tick/launchd.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/Users/junkawasaki/.gftd/orgs-detector-tick/launchd.log</string>

  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Standard</string>
</dict>
</plist>
